      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Leafs",
        "body": "The Maple Leafs' season didn't end with a bang - it ended with a whimper, a seven-game losing streak that turned late-season hope into the familiar ache of another unfulfilled promise. <strong>Toronto finished 32-36-14, eighth in the Atlantic Division with 78 points, 31 games behind the pace-setters, their playoff dreams buried under the weight of inconsistency and missed opportunities.</strong> This wasn't a team that collapsed in the stretch; this was a team that never quite got off the ground, limping across the finish line 15th in playoff positioning - a seat reserved for franchises in transition, not contenders.\n\nThe question now isn't what went wrong in May. It's what went wrong all season. Coaching stability, roster construction, depth scoring - all of it will be scrutinized in the coming weeks. Management faces hard choices: whether the current infrastructure can be salvaged or if a more fundamental reset is required. <strong>The draft lottery looms, and with it, the chance to infuse youth into a core that's proven it can't carry the load alone.</strong> Toronto's offseason will define whether this is a detour or a dead end.",
        "updated": "Updated May 11, 2026"
      },
