    "jays": {
      "full_name": "Toronto Blue Jays",
      "league": "MLB",
      "record": "18-22",
      "detail": "3rd in AL East",
      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Jays",
        "body": "Jo Adell crushed two solo shots into the Rogers Centre gloom, and suddenly the Blue Jays' sweep dreams evaporated - <strong>Angels 6, Blue Jays 1</strong> on Sunday, handing Toronto a flat-footed finish to what should've been a series rout. After torching L.A. 14-1 and 2-0 in the first two, the Jays watched José Soriano mow down 20 straight batters, his 7 2/3 innings of one-run mastery leaving their bats comatose post-first-inning RBI double from Kazuma Okamoto. Now sitting at 18-22, third in the AL East but <strong>8.5 games back</strong>, this L1 skid underscores a middling trajectory: too many nights where the offense sputters after early promise. Eric Lauer's 6-6 implosion - five innings, six earned - exposed the rotation's fragility, and with no fresh injury buzz lighting up the wires, it's on the lineup to claw back momentum. The skid dates back to that 0-3 Rays loss on May 6; time to snap it before the hole deepens. <strong>Tonight's 6:07 ET rematch with Tampa Bay</strong> looms large - win, and it's back to.500-ish contention whispers; drop it, and the AL East rearview grows crowded fast.",
        "updated": "Updated May 11, 2026"
      },
