      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Commanders",
        "body": "The draft dust settles in Ashburn, and the Washington Commanders emerge with a <strong>B- haul</strong> that screams calculated ambition - not fireworks, but fuses lit under Jayden Daniels' third-year arm. They pounced on Clemson's Antonio Williams at No. 71, a savvy slot wizard with chain-moving instincts primed to slot in Day 1 alongside Terry McLaurin, shoving Dyami Brown and Luke McCaffrey to the fringes in a receiver room begging for depth after last season's 5-12 slog, third in the NFC East but miles from contention. Ranked second in talent acquired and first in equity spent, GM Adam Peters flipped picks for a mid-sixth-round gem while UDFAs landed a dismal 30th - quantity over quality there. Williams fits Dan Quinn's scheme like a glove: quick releases, pro-ready routes to diversify the attack for Daniels, who's got $50 million in cap space to chase vets like Stefon Diggs, the Maryland homecoming king buzzing as a best fit. No more one-trick Terry; this rookie class injects balance. <strong>Watch OTAs next month</strong> - if Williams clicks, Daniels' leap becomes Washington's offseason win, vaulting them from pretenders to East disruptors.",
        "updated": "Updated May 11, 2026"
      },
